{
  "id": "artikel-oder-nicht",
  "name": "Article or no article?",
  "level": "A2",
  "status": "geprueft",
  "variety": "es-ES",
  "es419": { "note": "These rules hold in Latin America too. Words differ (coche: carro, auto; móvil: celular), not the article pattern." },
  "subtitle": "Spanish uses el, la, los, las for things in general with gustar: Me gusta el café. With tener, comprar and beber, the plain noun is normal: Tengo coche. Bebo agua. No article for jobs and languages: Es médica. Hablo español.",
  "briefHtml": "<p>Why “Me gusta el café” but “Bebo café”? Why “Es médica” with no article?</p><ul class=\"vp-brief-rules\"><li><b class=\"vp-brief-form vp-brief-form--none\">—</b><span>No article: jobs, languages, tener or beber + a general thing.</span></li><li><b class=\"vp-brief-form vp-brief-form--ein\">un, una</b><span>One of many, or a new thing: hay una farmacia.</span></li><li><b class=\"vp-brief-form vp-brief-form--der\">el, la</b><span>The one we both know, things in general with gustar, days of the week.</span></li></ul>",
  "briefMarks": ["el", "la", "los", "las", "un", "una"],
  "source": "icanspeak Spanish original text (grammar-a2 run, 2026-11-04)",
  "concept": {
    "flow": [
      { "text": "gustar, encantar + el, la, los, las", "variant": "prep" },
      { "text": "tener, beber, comprar + noun", "variant": "verb" },
      { "text": "ser + job · hablar + language" }
    ],
    "exampleHtml": "Me gusta <span class=\"vp-prep\">el</span> café. · Bebo <span class=\"vp-verb\">café</span>. · Tengo <span class=\"vp-verb\">coche</span>. · Es <span class=\"vp-verb\">médica</span>. · Hablo <span class=\"vp-verb\">español</span>. · <span class=\"vp-prep\">El</span> lunes voy al médico.",
    "qa": [
      {
        "html": "<strong>With gustar</strong> and verbs like it, the noun takes an article, even in general: <em>Me gusta el café. No me gustan los perros.</em> General subjects too: <em>El tabaco es malo. Los niños necesitan dormir.</em>",
        "tag": "gustar"
      },
      {
        "html": "<strong>With an ordinary verb</strong>, an amount of something or a general thing has no article: <em>Bebo café. Compro pan. Necesito ayuda. No tengo tiempo.</em>",
        "tag": "Bebo café"
      },
      {
        "html": "<strong>tener, buscar, llevar</strong> + the things people normally have one of, no article: <em>Tengo coche. Busco piso. No tengo móvil.</em> Add <em>un</em> when you describe it: <em>Tengo un coche viejo.</em>",
        "tag": "Tengo coche"
      },
      {
        "html": "<strong>Jobs after ser</strong>: no article. <em>Soy enfermera. Es médico.</em> With an adjective, add <em>un, una</em>: <em>Es una enfermera muy buena.</em> Also nationality and religion: <em>Es francés.</em>",
        "tag": "Jobs"
      },
      {
        "html": "<strong>Languages</strong> after <em>hablar, estudiar, saber</em>: usually no article. <em>Hablo español.</em> As a subject, with an article: <em>El español es fácil.</em>",
        "tag": "Languages"
      },
      {
        "html": "<strong>Always with an article:</strong> days (<em>el lunes</em>), times (<em>a las tres</em>), and titles when you talk about someone (<em>La señora López no está</em>). No article when you speak <em>to</em> them: <em>Buenos días, señora López.</em> And <em>hay</em> never takes <em>el, la</em>: <em>Hay una farmacia.</em>",
        "tag": "Always el, la"
      }
    ],
    "noteHtml": "Common mistakes: ✗ <em>Me gusta café</em> → ✓ <em>Me gusta el café</em>. ✗ <em>Soy un médico</em> → ✓ <em>Soy médico</em>. ✗ <em>Hablo el español</em> → ✓ <em>Hablo español</em>. ✗ <em>Hay la farmacia</em> → ✓ <em>Hay una farmacia</em>. ✗ <em>Voy en lunes</em> → ✓ <em>Voy el lunes</em>.",
    "reference": {
      "showLabel": "Show overview ▼",
      "hideLabel": "Hide overview ▲",
      "tables": [
